  "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n* If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on $(a,b).$\n\n* If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on $(a,b).$\n\nThe graph below shows that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-3,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14202093/assets/c72ad85a6998493f29289074a4750f2830e8fdefe51d6f81536257abfb8ae008.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly increasing when $x\\in (-3,3).$",
